# Compiled binaries. `go build` in a module directory drops one next to the
# source, and a 3 MB binary in a commit is both noise in review and permanent
# weight in the history -- this repository already had to be rewritten once to
# remove one.
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*.test

# The same binaries under a non-Windows GOOS, which carry no extension and so
# are not matched above. A cross-build for the release matrix drops one of
# these next to the source, and `git add -A` then stages a 4 MB file that
# reviews as a single unreadable line.
/cmd/pglogwatch/pglogwatch
/bench/cmd/compare/compare
/bench/cmd/corpus/corpus

# Coverage and profiling output.
coverage.out
*.prof
*.pprof

# The generated benchmark corpus. DAT-001: the manifest is committed, the
# payload is regenerated by `make corpus`.
/bench/corpus/

# The fuzzer writes here, and what it writes is a crasher: an input that
# just failed. Those are ignored so that a failing run does not leave a
# staged file behind for somebody to commit without reading it.
#
# The reviewed corpus under this path is committed anyway, because git
# ignores only untracked files -- so `git add -f` once makes those files
# tracked forever and every later crasher still lands ignored. FUZZING.md
# records what is in there and how it got there.
/testdata/fuzz/

# The comparative results table. TST-009 publishes it as a build artifact; a
# copy committed per run would accumulate numbers nobody re-reads, and a stale
# one is worse than none.
/bench/RESULTS.md

# What bench/scaling-baseline.sh writes: profiles, a test binary and a log,
# dropped in the working directory so a run on the benchmark machine needs no
# argument. Same reasoning as bench/RESULTS.md -- the numbers belong in
# bench/THRESHOLDS.md once read, not in the history once per run.
/scaling-out/
scaling-baseline.log
